{"qid":"Q62389","names":["Icelandic Commonwealth"],"title":"Icelandic Commonwealth","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Icelandic Commonwealth (Þjóðveldið) was a decentralized republican polity established in 930 with the founding of the Althing at Þingvellir — the world's oldest surviving parliament — following Norse settlement of Iceland begun around 874. It lacked a king or executive authority; governance rested on 36 (later 39) chieftains called goðar whose power was checked by law-speakers and the general assembly. Chronic aristocratic feuding in the Age of the Sturlungs (1220s–1260s) made the system ungovernable; in 1262–1264 the major chieftains submitted to King Hákon IV of Norway under the Old Covenant (Gamli sáttmáli), incorporating Iceland into the Norwegian crown.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Iceland's territory is the island itself; internal goðorð boundaries are reconstructed from saga literature and are approximate.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Iceland — The Commonwealth Period","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Iceland/The-Commonwealth-period-930-1262"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Icelandic Commonwealth","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Icelandic_Commonwealth"},{"label":"Britannica: Althing","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Althing"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Icelandic Commonwealth","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Hrafn Hœngsson","nativeName":"Hrafn Hœngsson","roman":"Hrafn Hœngsson","start":930,"end":949,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Thorarin Ragabrodir","nativeName":"Þórarinn Óleifsson ragabróðir","ro